WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.989
A Brazilian distributor reads electronic invoices with a model.

00:00:06.289 --> 00:00:10.804
Every company in Brazil has to issue them, so there are thousands a day.

00:00:11.454 --> 00:00:13.392
The prompt asks for a lot.

00:00:13.692 --> 00:00:15.502
Extract the line items.

00:00:15.802 --> 00:00:21.079
Add them up, apply the discount, and check the result against the invoice total.

00:00:21.379 --> 00:00:23.536
Compute each item's state tax.

00:00:23.836 --> 00:00:25.115
Convert the dates.

00:00:25.415 --> 00:00:28.643
And check that the issuer's tax ID is valid.

00:00:29.293 --> 00:00:33.378
Every few hundred invoices, something comes out slightly wrong.

00:00:33.678 --> 00:00:36.184
The sum is wrong by a few cents.

00:00:36.484 --> 00:00:39.046
One item's tax is rounded differently.

00:00:39.346 --> 00:00:43.333
Or an invalid tax ID gets marked as valid.

00:00:43.983 --> 00:00:45.725
The team can't find a pattern.

00:00:46.025 --> 00:00:47.242
Because there isn't one.

00:00:47.542 --> 00:00:49.726
It's the model doing arithmetic.

00:00:50.026 --> 00:00:55.595
In this lesson, you'll learn to spot this smell, why it hides so well, and how to

00:00:55.595 --> 00:00:59.308
split the work so the math is right every single time.

00:01:00.508 --> 00:01:02.860
Let's look at what's really happening here.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.853
The document arrives.

00:01:05.503 --> 00:01:09.042
The model does two different things in one call.

00:01:09.342 --> 00:01:11.697
Understand the document, and do math.

00:01:12.347 --> 00:01:18.214
And the math is generated token by token, as text, with no guarantee of being exact.

00:01:18.864 --> 00:01:21.330
Almost always, the result matches.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.672
That almost is the whole problem.

00:01:24.322 --> 00:01:26.354
Why would a model get simple math wrong?

00:01:26.654 --> 00:01:28.188
Because it doesn't add.

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:29.850
It predicts the next token.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.645
A number like one thousand two hundred eighty-four, with cents, becomes a few

00:01:34.645 --> 00:01:37.915
tokens, and they're not always split in the same places.

00:01:38.215 --> 00:01:42.503
To add, the model writes digits that look plausible after what came before.

00:01:43.153 --> 00:01:46.336
Most of the time, plausible and correct are the same thing.

00:01:46.636 --> 00:01:50.811
Current models get far more math right than the ones from a few years ago.

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:53.284
But far more isn't always.

00:01:53.584 --> 00:01:56.074
And a tax system needs always.

00:01:56.724 --> 00:01:57.635
How do you spot it?

00:01:57.935 --> 00:02:03.854
The prompt asks to calculate, add, convert, format, or validate something that has

00:02:03.854 --> 00:02:04.865
an exact rule.

00:02:05.515 --> 00:02:08.717
The errors are rare, small, and have no pattern.

00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:09.693
A cent.

00:02:09.993 --> 00:02:10.644
A digit.

00:02:10.944 --> 00:02:11.551
A day.

00:02:12.201 --> 00:02:15.956
The same document gives different results on different runs.

00:02:16.606 --> 00:02:21.880
And somewhere in the system, there's code that redoes the math to double-check, and

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.151
sometimes disagrees.

00:02:23.451 --> 00:02:27.374
If you already wrote the function, why is the model doing it too?

00:02:28.574 --> 00:02:33.297
Writing a sentence in the prompt is faster than writing a function with tests.

00:02:33.947 --> 00:02:36.508
The model gets it right in testing.

00:02:36.808 --> 00:02:40.239
With five sample invoices, the math comes out perfect.

00:02:40.539 --> 00:02:42.462
The error shows up at scale.

00:02:43.112 --> 00:02:46.948
The line between understanding and calculating is blurry.

00:02:47.248 --> 00:02:50.878
Extract the total sounds like calculate the total.

00:02:51.178 --> 00:02:51.707
It isn't.

00:02:52.007 --> 00:02:54.716
The first is reading a number that's printed.

00:02:55.016 --> 00:02:57.232
The second is producing a new one.

00:02:57.882 --> 00:03:00.537
And rules nobody wants to maintain.

00:03:00.837 --> 00:03:06.571
Validating a tax ID or counting business days takes code and tests.

00:03:06.871 --> 00:03:10.493
Pushing it into the prompt feels like skipping that work.

00:03:11.693 --> 00:03:13.270
The cost starts with money.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:17.610
One wrong cent on an invoice creates accounting rework.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.685
At scale, it's trouble with the tax authority.

00:03:21.335 --> 00:03:22.504
Then trust.

00:03:22.804 --> 00:03:28.001
When the error is rare and random, nobody trusts any result, and everything gets

00:03:28.001 --> 00:03:29.040
checked by hand.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:31.284
Then tokens.

00:03:31.584 --> 00:03:35.617
Output tokens usually cost several times more than input.

00:03:35.917 --> 00:03:39.032
You're paying for math that one line of code does for free.

00:03:39.682 --> 00:03:41.416
Then flaky tests.

00:03:41.716 --> 00:03:47.411
The same case passes and fails on different runs, and the team learns to ignore red

00:03:47.411 --> 00:03:48.509
in the pipeline.

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:50.395
And rules change.

00:03:50.695 --> 00:03:56.484
Since July twenty twenty-six, new Brazilian company tax IDs can contain letters.

00:03:56.784 --> 00:04:00.715
A rule like that has to be updated in one place, with a test.

00:04:01.015 --> 00:04:06.434
In a prompt, you find out about the change when the model starts rejecting valid

00:04:06.434 --> 00:04:06.705
IDs.

00:04:07.905 --> 00:04:09.379
Here's one for you.

00:04:09.679 --> 00:04:11.844
Your team switched to a bigger model.

00:04:12.144 --> 00:04:17.439
The invoice sum now fails once every two thousand invoices, instead of once every

00:04:17.439 --> 00:04:18.354
three hundred.

00:04:18.654 --> 00:04:20.722
The manager wants to call it solved.

00:04:21.022 --> 00:04:21.920
What do you say?

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:28.355
That the math has an exact rule, and should leave the model.

00:04:28.655 --> 00:04:30.371
A better rate isn't a guarantee.

00:04:30.671 --> 00:04:35.982
A bigger model errs less, but it still produces digits by prediction.

00:04:36.282 --> 00:04:40.344
A sum function is always right, and costs almost nothing.

00:04:40.994 --> 00:04:45.573
And a rare error in a tax amount is worse than a frequent one, in a way.

00:04:45.873 --> 00:04:49.522
It's more expensive to find, precisely because it's rare.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:54.064
Asking the model to double-check its own sum doesn't help either.

00:04:54.364 --> 00:04:58.020
The check runs on the same mechanism that made the mistake.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:05.434
The treatment is a refactoring called replace LLM call with code.

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:08.326
And the rule for splitting the work is simple.

00:05:08.626 --> 00:05:11.081
The model copies what's written in the document.

00:05:11.381 --> 00:05:13.284
Code produces any new number.

00:05:13.934 --> 00:05:18.718
So the model's output becomes structured data with only the values it read.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:25.115
And notice they come back as text, exactly as printed, with the comma Brazil uses

00:05:25.115 --> 00:05:26.094
for decimals.

00:05:26.394 --> 00:05:32.575
The tax ID, the date, each item's quantity, unit price and tax rate, the discount,

00:05:32.575 --> 00:05:34.234
and the printed total.

00:05:34.884 --> 00:05:38.620
Then code converts, multiplies, adds and compares.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:44.050
One hundred twenty boxes at three dollars fifteen is three hundred seventy-eight.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:48.700
Thirty-six rolls of tape at seven ninety is two hundred eighty-four forty.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.552
The sum, minus the discount, is six hundred thirty-seven forty.

00:05:53.852 --> 00:05:57.195
But the invoice prints six hundred thirty-four forty.

00:05:57.845 --> 00:06:01.355
Before, the model might have checked and said it matched.

00:06:01.655 --> 00:06:05.009
Now the mismatch is a calculated fact.

00:06:05.309 --> 00:06:08.482
The invoice goes to review, with the reason attached.

00:06:09.132 --> 00:06:10.567
And two details matter.

00:06:10.867 --> 00:06:14.170
For money, use a decimal type, never floating point.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:18.212
And the tax rounding rule is the tax team's decision.

00:06:18.512 --> 00:06:20.989
Now it's written somewhere you can test.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:26.617
Now a colleague suggests: let's have the model return each line amount already

00:06:26.617 --> 00:06:28.531
multiplied, to save some code.

00:06:28.831 --> 00:06:30.476
Why is that a step back?

00:06:30.776 --> 00:06:34.902
Because the multiplication goes back to being a number the model produced.

00:06:35.202 --> 00:06:39.774
The split only works if the model never produces a new number.

00:06:40.074 --> 00:06:42.100
Even a simple multiplication.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:44.643
When can you ignore it?

00:06:44.943 --> 00:06:45.769
Estimates.

00:06:46.069 --> 00:06:50.434
Roughly how many pages is this report doesn't need to be exact.

00:06:51.084 --> 00:06:53.630
Judgment, with no exact rule.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:57.856
Deciding whether a comment is offensive is the model's job.

00:06:58.156 --> 00:07:03.359
If it disagrees with itself on borderline cases, that's expected, not this smell.

00:07:04.009 --> 00:07:05.413
And a prototype.

00:07:05.713 --> 00:07:09.664
To check that extraction works, letting the model add is fine.

00:07:09.964 --> 00:07:11.507
Take it out before production.

00:07:12.157 --> 00:07:15.829
And watch for the smell hiding inside a conversation.

00:07:16.129 --> 00:07:19.623
An assistant answers customers about delivery dates.

00:07:19.923 --> 00:07:23.908
The prompt says: add five business days to the purchase date.

00:07:24.208 --> 00:07:26.348
Sometimes it counts a holiday.

00:07:26.648 --> 00:07:31.198
It's a chat with a customer, but the calculation inside it is deterministic.

00:07:31.498 --> 00:07:36.405
Listing the holidays in the prompt keeps the math in the model, just with more text.

00:07:36.705 --> 00:07:39.900
A date tool solves it, and the model explains the result.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:42.899
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:43.199 --> 00:07:47.685
If the operation has an exact rule, it doesn't belong in a prompt.

00:07:47.985 --> 00:07:53.763
Let the model do what only it can do, read a messy document, and let code produce

00:07:53.763 --> 00:07:54.976
every new number.

00:07:55.276 --> 00:07:59.435
Then a mismatch stops being noise, and becomes a useful alarm.

00:08:00.085 --> 00:08:05.768
In the next lesson, we'll do that refactoring step by step, with tests that pin the

00:08:05.768 --> 00:08:08.370
rule down: replace LLM call with code.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:09.499
See you there.
